ABSTRACT_EN = [
'Sensor-fault handling in automated driving stacks largely relies on a single strategy — an immediate in-lane stop — which leaves the vehicle obstructing traffic, while undetected faults let the vehicle keep driving with degraded perception. This thesis proposes a graded fault-response framework consisting of (1) a behavior-tree-based monitor (cdr_monitor) that estimates per-sensor health as continuous scores from freshness, quality, and physics channels, and (2) a supervisor (mrm_supervisor) that selects between shoulder/parking-lot evacuation (MRM_MOVE) and deferral to the stock stop (MRM_DEFER) according to fault type, severity, and remaining capability, and enforces a conservative fixed speed cap (3.0 m/s, below the model-derived upper bound of about 3.89 m/s obtained from a stopping-distance model under assumed nominal parameters d = vτ + v²/2a) during evacuation; a health-proportional cap is implemented but was not used in the reported experiments.',
'The framework was evaluated in a closed loop of Autoware 0.39.0 and CARLA 0.9.16 (Town07, 15 km/h speed limit). A matrix of 1,131 valid cases across fault types, severities, traffic densities, and methods verified the monitoring and evacuation behavior: the detector achieved 96-100% detection rates in 11 of 12 fault conditions, with median detection latencies of about 1.0 s for hard fails (ledger re-measurement) and 2.1-5.0 s for degradation faults (the exception being mild LiDAR sparsification that is tolerated by design) with zero false activations in 45 fault-free cases, and supervision reduced degraded-state driving exposure from 238-275 m (unsupervised stock) to 32-58 m (detect + evacuate, camera and localization faults). In the ablation sample, the in-lane stop kept a median post-encounter moving exposure of 0.0 m while occupying the driving lane beyond the observation horizon, whereas evacuation spent a median of 37.2 m of controlled low-speed motion. Among the 20 evacuation-arm cases (seeds S02-S11, arms A3/A4; non-ablation cases — development seeds and ladder runs — and post-collision departures were excluded by the inclusion criteria defined in the analysis rules of Chapter 4), 7 collided and are reported separately as failed lane clearance, 6 cleared the driving lane within the observation horizon (observed median 11.0 s), and 7 did not clear within the horizon (right-censored); with clearance observed in 6 of 13 collision-free cases, the Kaplan-Meier survival curve does not fall below 0.5 (terminal survival 0.538), so the median clearance time is not reached within the horizon. Safety was quantified at the encounter level with a triple collision-verdict system — a real-time ego collision-sensor ledger (RAW-HIT), a walker-physics oracle, and a human visual cross-check protocol — introduced after empirically discovering that a walker-attached collision sensor never fired; the pitfalls of runner exit labels and official infraction records are documented as a methodological contribution.',
'Distance-ladder experiments located the avoid/collide boundary at 8-11 m for 4.2 m/s driving (consistent with the stopping-distance model prediction of 8.8 m) and at 7-8 m for 3.0 m/s. In a paired same-seed four-arm ablation (20 seeds over two identically-protocoled rounds, pedestrian dart-out at 8 m launched at detection+3 s, i.e., during the deceleration transient), native continuation collided in 14/20 seeds, detection with in-lane stop in 0/20, uncapped evacuation in 10/20, and nominally capped (3.0 m/s) evacuation in 11/20 (exact McNemar, native continuation (A1) vs capped evacuation (A4): p=0.375). A post-campaign audit found that the explicit cap had never been delivered to the planning layer (a message-type mismatch silently discarded on the same topic name), so both evacuation arms were de facto the same policy; the cap was fixed (type match + periodic republish) and its effect was then tested causally in a dose-response experiment restricted to settled encounters where the cap actually held (3 caps x 4 spawn distances x 10 seeds = 120 runs, settlement-state trigger, pre-registered primary endpoint): at 8 m, cap 4.2 vs 3.0 m/s gave discordant pairs 10:0 (exact McNemar p=0.002), and the avoid/collide boundary moved inward monotonically with lower caps (beyond 9 m to about 7 m). The same low-speed evacuation policy launched in the deceleration transient remains at risk, and the low-speed settled cruise avoided all encounters (0/8). The MRM choice is thus a temporal trade-off between immediate collision risk (favoring the stop) and the possibility, when evacuation succeeds, of clearing the lane to an off-lane refuge position, and the residual risk of evacuation concentrates in the detection-to-deceleration transient (11/20 transient vs 0/8 settled; a single launch instant at detection + 3 s) — an observational two-regime comparison, not a causal manipulation of detection timing, that nevertheless provides quantitative observational evidence supporting the need for earlier detection and faster deceleration in widening the safety margin within the fault-tolerant time interval (FTTI). Hard failures (complete signal loss) are decided through an evidence-aware fast path that skips noise-oriented debounce and futile recovery holds, meeting a 1.5-s detection-to-decision deadline across the full cf/lf matrix (max 1.20 s, simulation-time, ledger-based). A supplementary campaign extended the fault model to correlated GNSS bias, GNSS outage, and IMU bias/drift faults (611 valid cases, 100% detection in all conditions). Limitations include the single map, the single 15 km/h speed limit, two launch corridors, and the version-specific scope of all Autoware observations.',
]
ABSTRACT_KEYWORDS = 'Keywords: autonomous vehicle, fail-safe, functional safety, minimal risk maneuver, sensor fault detection, FTTI'
